WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:09.263
The card-issuer API takes 120 ms, and you want to time the client method that calls it.

2
00:00:09.263 --> 00:00:13.691
But that client lives in a library jar you can't recompile.

3
00:00:13.867 --> 00:00:15.027
A proxy won't do.

4
00:00:15.027 --> 00:00:20.280
It needs an interface or an open class, and it only wraps objects you create.

5
00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:24.666
Every class reaches the JVM as bytes from a jar.

6
00:00:24.666 --> 00:00:29.315
A class loader hands them over, and the JVM defines the class.

7
00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:36.708
Start the JVM with -javaagent, and before your main runs, it calls the agent's premain method.

8
00:00:36.933 --> 00:00:39.863
premain registers a ClassFileTransformer.

9
00:00:39.863 --> 00:00:45.437
From then on, the JVM passes it the bytes of every class it's about to define.

10
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.455
It returns new bytes, or null for no change.

11
00:00:48.455 --> 00:00:52.867
Here it adds timing code to IssuerClient, and leaves the rest alone.

12
00:00:53.733 --> 00:00:56.785
Now take a service that has run for an hour.

13
00:00:56.785 --> 00:01:00.809
A tool attaches an agent, and the JVM calls its agentmain.

14
00:01:00.809 --> 00:01:02.820
IssuerClient loaded long ago.

15
00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:05.387
Will the new transformer ever see it?

16
00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:07.686
No.

17
00:01:07.686 --> 00:01:15.139
A transformer only sees classes defined after it's registered, and nothing defines IssuerClient again.

18
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:18.045
So the agent calls retransformClasses.

19
00:01:18.045 --> 00:01:26.677
The JVM runs the transformers again on the class's current bytes, and puts the new method bodies into the same Class object.

20
00:01:27.867 --> 00:01:31.418
Objects made at startup run the new code on their next call.

21
00:01:31.418 --> 00:01:34.792
A call that was already running finishes on the old code.

22
00:01:35.533 --> 00:01:38.468
One limit: only method bodies may change.

23
00:01:38.468 --> 00:01:46.629
Retransformation must not add, remove or rename fields or methods, or change signatures, modifiers or inheritance.

24
00:01:46.629 --> 00:01:49.421
So a call counter can't be a new field.

25
00:01:49.421 --> 00:01:51.283
It lives in another class.

26
00:01:52.067 --> 00:01:58.559
In code, that's two calls, the same in Java and in Kotlin, and the agent's manifest must allow it.

27
00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:04.348
The rule: a transformer sees each class's bytes as the JVM defines it.

28
00:02:04.348 --> 00:02:11.099
Retransformation swaps method bodies in place, for every object, but never the class's shape.

29
00:02:11.267 --> 00:02:12.081
Quick check.

30
00:02:12.081 --> 00:02:16.900
An agent retransforms IssuerClient while a thread is inside its method.

31
00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.479
Which code does that thread finish on?

32
00:02:21.867 --> 00:02:22.835
The old code.

33
00:02:22.835 --> 00:02:25.144
Its next call runs the new one.
